In command injection and vulnerability research, structural containers (often called command separators, operators, or substitution mechanisms) are characters or sequences used to change how a shell or runtime environment interprets text.
When a backend application passes user input to a system terminal, these containers are used to break out of the intended data context and force the execution of arbitrary operating system commands.
Below is every major structural container that exists across Linux/Unix and Windows environments.
------------------------------
## I. Command Substitution Containers
These containers tell the shell to execute the enclosed command first, and then place its text output directly into the surrounding command line. [1] 

| Container | Operating System | Description / Example |
|---|---|---|
| `command` | Linux / Unix | Backticks: Evaluates the text inside as a shell command. Used in older POSIX standards. Example: echo \whoami`` |
| $(command) | Linux / Unix / PowerShell | POSIX Substitution: Modern replacement for backticks. Allows nesting of multiple command layers. Example: echo $(whoami) |
| $<command> | Older Unix Shells | Alternative Substitution: Rare syntax used in older or specialized shell variants to capture command streams. |

------------------------------
## II. Sequential & Conditional Operators (Inline Block Containers)
These containers allow you to chain multiple commands together on a single line, running them either sequentially or based on the success of the previous command.

| Container | Operating System | Description / Example |
|---|---|---|
| ; | Linux / Unix | Semicolon: Runs the second command unconditionally after the first one finishes. Example: id; whoami |
| %0A / \n | Linux / Windows | Newline Character: Forces the shell interpreter to treat the next string as a completely new command line. Example: id%0Awhoami |
| %0D / \r | Linux / Windows | Carriage Return: Similar to a newline, it cuts off the current input buffer and starts a fresh instruction line. |
| && | Linux / Windows | Logical AND: Runs the second command only if the first command succeeds (returns exit code 0). Example: ping -c 1 127.0.0.1 && whoami |
| || | Linux / Windows | Logical OR: Runs the second command only if the first command fails (returns a non-zero exit code). Example: false || whoami |

------------------------------
## III. Data Stream & Piping Containers
These containers hijack the input and output channels of the terminal, sending data from one process into another or redirecting files. [2, 3] 

| Container | Operating System | Description / Example |
|---|---|---|
| | | Linux / Windows | Pipe: Takes the standard output text of the left command and feeds it as the direct input to the right command. Example: cat /etc/passwd | grep root |
| > | Linux / Windows | Output Redirection: Overwrites a file on disk with the output text of your command. Example: whoami > /tmp/output.txt |
| >> | Linux / Windows | Append Redirection: Appends the command output to the end of a file without erasing existing contents. |
| < | Linux / Unix | Input Redirection: Forces a command to read its arguments from a specified file instead of user input. Example: cat < /etc/passwd |

------------------------------
## IV. Mathematical & Arithmetic Evaluation Containers
These containers force the shell into a mathematical or logical execution state. In complex scenarios, these states can be broken out of to trigger binary executions. [4] 

| Container | Operating System | Description / Example |
|---|---|---|
| $((expression)) | Linux / Unix | Arithmetic Expansion: Evaluates standard mathematical operations. Can nest shell variables or calls. Example: echo $((1+1)) |
| $[expression] | Older Linux / Bash | Deprecated Arithmetic: An older syntax for mathematical expansion still supported on older Linux builds. |

------------------------------
## V. Target-Specific Windows Inline Containers
Windows environments (CMD and PowerShell) feature unique variable and command processing containers distinct from Linux environments. [5] 

| Container | Environment | Description / Example |
|---|---|---|
| %variable% | Windows CMD | Environment Variables: Expands internal Windows properties. Can be used to concatenate strings. Example: %COMSPEC% (points to cmd.exe) |
| & | Windows CMD | Command Separator: The Windows equivalent of the Unix semicolon. Runs commands sequentially. Example: dir & whoami |

