## Pro-Tip for Triage
When analyzing the ?code=YOURCODE%60 endpoint, you are looking to see if the web application's backend collapses when encountering these operators. If you swap out the trailing backtick (%60) for a URL-encoded newline (%0A) or a semicolon (%3B), you can observe whether the application throws a different error code or changes its response layout.
If you test these characters on your target container or environment, let me know:

* Does substituting the backtick with a semicolon (%3B) or newline (%0A) alter the server behavior?
* Do you notice any changes in error patterns between them?

